[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65200,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Papillary hidradenoma is a benign tumour characterized by branching and interconnecting epithelial and stromal elements.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65201,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8405/0 Papillary hidradenoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65202,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F22 &amp; XH4DX4 Benign neoplasms of epidermal appendages &amp; Papillary hidradenoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65203,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> hidradenoma papilliferum</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65204,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65205,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Papillary hidradenoma is most commonly seen in the interlabial sulcus or labia majora {26944596}. See the WHO classification of skin tumours [[Elder DE, Massi D, Scolyer RA, et al., editors. WHO classification of skin tumours. Lyon (France): International Agency for Research on Cancer; 2018. (WHO classification of tumours series, 4th ed.; vol. 11). https://publications.iarc.fr/560.]].</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65206,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Papillary hidradenoma most commonly affects women in the third or fourth decade of life. The usual presentation is with a solitary, firm mass, sometimes with overlying ulceration and bleeding. The lesion is well circumscribed and mobile. The overlying epidermis is usually normal.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65207,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>This is the most common benign glandular neoplasm of the vulva. Papillary hidradenoma almost exclusively affects women aged 20&ndash;89&nbsp;years {10411423}. It is extremely rare in children {10986678}. Hidradenoma papilliferum is mainly identified in white populations; it is rare in black populations.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-30T12:16:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65208,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65209,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These lesions are thought to arise from anogenital mammary-like glands, normal constituents of the vulva (especially interlabial sulcus), perineal areas, and perianal areas. Tumorigenic alterations in the PI3K/AKT pathway have been identified as the most prevalent genetic aberration {31010589; 26493284; 28648934}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65210,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumour is typically a tan or red nodule with a solid to variably cystic cut surface, measuring on average 1.5&nbsp;cm.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-30T12:16:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65211,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumour is composed of complex branching and interconnecting tubules and papillae consisting of central delicate fibrovascular cores lined by uniform, cuboidal to columnar secretory cells and underlying myoepithelial cells. Oxyphilic metaplasia is common. Mitotic activity is often present and is occasionally high {26863059; 16871035}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65212,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65213,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65214,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> complex growth of branching and interconnecting epithelial tubules and papillae, with subepithelial myoepithelial cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65215,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:25:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65216,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 376,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "<p>This is a benign tumour, but it may recur locally if incompletely excised. Cases of carcinoma in&nbsp;situ resembling ductal carcinoma in&nbsp;situ of the breast and low-grade phyllodes tumour arising in papillary hidradenoma have been reported {18645314}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-30T12:16:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]